{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RESOLVED as not_a_bug — /rebar-debug Phase 1 only; the not-a-bug guard held so Phase 2 never unlocked. No code changed; the 480e worktree was removed unused.\n\nProven mechanism (runtime oracle, not source reading): sampled refs/reconciler/lock every 10s for 2 minutes. The blob was byte-identical across all 12 samples — same oid 6e5b4468, heartbeat frozen at 1785386477382441825 (04:41:17), lease_secs 120. A frozen heartbeat means the holder was not renewing, so the lease expired at 04:43:17.\n\nDiscriminating experiment at lease age 220s:\n- H1 (check_pass_lock honors lease expiry) predicted False.\n- H2 (returns True whenever the ref exists; stale lock blocks forever) predicted True.\nResult: check_pass_lock -> False. H1 confirmed, H2 rejected.\n\nThe guard is therefore correct in both directions: held while the lease was live, free once expired. Every earlier refusal coincided with a genuinely live lease (the first hit one at 117s of 120s). No live-lock, no stale holder, no always-held condition. Classification: intended-behavior — the correct action was to wait out the pass.\n\nWithdrawn: the claim that fsck --repair is affected. It shares the same correct function.\n\nMy original misdiagnosis: I grepped for the import line, saw that import fail in a bare interpreter, and concluded the probe could never read the lock — without reading the four lines above it that insert engine_dir() into sys.path first. I asserted a mechanism from a partial read instead of observing it run.\n\nSurviving observation (not this ticket): _reconciler_in_flight has no test coverage."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785387668381388001,"uuid":"c6d6de7c-557a-442d-9cbd-f0c56c937c6a"}